BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law] Frances Ca
 yton — Civic Consciousness and the Limits of Attacks on Civil Society
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T131500
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54003635337029-54003640246129@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Why do some backsliding incumbents co-opt civil society while 
 others fail? This paper introduces a theory of civic consciousness\, conte
 nding that\, as compared to their formal or political counterparts\, infor
 mal and apolitical civil society groups often fail to recognize their civi
 c resources. I show that government threats to associational freedom help 
 these groups recognize their latent social capital and increase their will
 ingness to mobilize in support of democracy. As evidence\, this paper dr\n
 \nAges: All ages\nSource: https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/frances-ca
 yton-civic-consciousness-and-limits-attacks-civil-society\nLast synced: 20
 26-09-28 13:01 PDT
URL:https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/frances-cayton-civic-consciousne
 ss-and-limits-attacks-civil-society
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Geophysics Department] Geophysics Seminar - Johan Robertsson\, "M
 aking waves: Immersive wave experimentation"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T130000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53993750112877-53993759042895@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Many applications in computational geophysics require modeling
  seismic wave propagation across a set of closely related subsurface model
 s. In such cases\, it is often preferable to recompute wavefields locally
 \, only in regions of change\, rather than across the full model. Immersiv
 e boundary conditions make this possible: they capture the full interactio
 n between two domains while requiring recomputation only within the domain
  that changes. The same methodology also enables immersing a physical ex\n
 \nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:Mitchell Earth Sciences
END:VEVENT
