BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Cantor Arts] Lunchtime Curator Talk | JANE!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T130000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53685756293663-53685759594051@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Join Patrick R. Crowley\, Associate Curator of European Art\, 
 on this special highlights tour of JANE!\n\nJANE! celebrates the aesthetic
  and affective excesses of Jane Stanford\, the Gilded Age founder and matr
 iarch of Stanford University. Bringing together precious keepsakes\, souve
 nirs\, archaeological detritus\, and spooky messages from beyond the grave
 \, the exhibition reveals the campy underside of a familiar biography and 
 perhaps an even more familiar murder mystery.\n\nFrom her elegant French g
 owns\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://thethirdplace.is/event/jane806\nLa
 st synced: 2026-09-28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center
URL:https://thethirdplace.is/event/jane806
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center for South Asia] Sober State: Origins of Alcohol Prohibitio
 n in India| Book Talk with Darinee Alagirisamy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T130000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53800058118106-53800058118107@local-events
DESCRIPTION:This event is sponsored by the Center for South Asia.\n\nAbout
  the event\nWhat happens when a democratic state―still in the process of
  formation―commits to banning a substance\, especially one as controvers
 ial as alcohol? This book traces the origins and evolution of alcohol proh
 ibition in India\, drawing on extensive archival research and rich vernacu
 lar sources to explain its surprising resilience over time. Since its ince
 ption\, prohibition has served both as an ideal and a tool of state power
 ―a du\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:Encina Commons
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Stanford Humanities Center] Justene Hill Edwards | Slavery\, Free
 dom\, and the Complicated Logics of American Capitalism
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T140000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54064324667679-54064324667680@local-events
DESCRIPTION:What is the relationship between capitalism and freedom in the
  United States? This question has motivated a generation of scholars who h
 ave looked to the history of American slavery to better understand the ori
 gins of American capitalism. But scholars have yet to fully grapple with t
 he ways in which enslaved people\, both as property and as economic agents
 \, understood the complicated logics of American capitalism. This presenta
 tion\, organized in collaboration with the Slavery and Freedom workshop\n
 \nAges: All ages\nSource: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdLZ3R3
 _9ICGzhYRgmWbN_eYouVJE5ORUFD9Jxy0OOdQmbe1A/viewform\nLast synced: 2026-09-
 28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:Humanities Center
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdLZ3R3_9ICGzhYRgmWbN_eYouVJE
 5ORUFD9Jxy0OOdQmbe1A/viewform
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[David Rumsey Map Center / Stanford University Libraries] Emergent
  Properties: Decennial Reflections on Collections
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T170000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53923391076842-53923487679349@local-events
DESCRIPTION:In 2026 the David Rumsey Map Center celebrates its tenth year
 —a moment to reflect on how the Center has added value to scholarship on
  campus and around the world. Our collections are vast and not bounded by 
 geography\, spanning the 15th century to the present. Yet over the last de
 cade\, certain materials have repeatedly resonated with the students\, ins
 tructors\, and researchers who frequent the Center. Emergent Properties br
 ings together a selection of highlights from deep within the collections o
 f\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:David Rumsey Map Center (Green Library)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Department of Art & Art History / Stanford Arts Institute] Tangli
 ng the Digital: Weaving\, Printmaking\, Computing
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261001T170000
DTSTAMP:20260928T200148Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53948654465100-53948654469199@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Digital objects are often seen as alien to craft\, as though n
 o hands had shaped them. They can be copied endlessly and exactly\, bereft
  of the maker’s touch. Yet weaving and printmaking are among our oldest 
 technologies of reproduction\, fixing patterns so they can be repeated and
  circulated. The printing press and the Jacquard loom initiated processes 
 of automation that displaced human hands—and that still animate our digi
 tal tools.\n\nWarp and weft describe not just a textile structure but a lo
 gic\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-28 13:01 PDT
LOCATION:McMurtry Building
END:VEVENT
